<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sophonisba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/203-bce">203 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Sophoniba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sophonisba was a Carthaginian noblewoman, daughter of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal Gisco</a>, who was married to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> of the Masaesyli to bind him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. After Syphax's capture in 203 BCE she married his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, but when Scipio demanded her surrender she took poison. Her story inspired many later tragedies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sophonisba.jpg" alt="Sophonisba" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SIMONE CANTARINI · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simone-cantarini---Sofonisba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Driven from his inheritance by Syphax, who had allied himself with Carthage by marrying the Carthaginian noblewoman <b>Sophonisba</b>, Masinissa joined Scipio's invasion of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He first made contact with Rome and received envoys from Scipio, but in 205 BCE married <b>Sophonisba</b>, daughter of the Carthaginian general Hasdrubal, and sided with Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/state/masaesyli">Masaesyli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-213" aria-label="Carthage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 836 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sophonisba"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-213">What was here in 213 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I accept this nuptial present; nor is it an unwelcome one, if my husband can render me no better service.&#8221;</p><footer>Livy, History of Rome, book 30 &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; on receiving the poison her husband Masinissa sent her so that she would not be led in a Roman triumph, 203 BCE; Livy's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
